\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {AllelePerturb defines perturbation prediction at protein-coding variant resolution.} \textbf  {a}, Conceptual distinction between gene-level and protein-coding variant-level perturbation prediction. Gene-level perturbation identity collapses responses from different variants of the same gene into a single averaged effect, whereas AllelePerturb treats a held-out protein-coding variant as the prediction target and asks whether models can distinguish allele-specific responses within the same gene. \textbf  {b}, Overview of AllelePerturb-Bench. The benchmark integrates 472 protein-coding variants across four genes and protein architectures (TP53, KRAS, GATA1 and JAK1), spanning three experimental technologies: Perturb-seq, base editing and scSNV-seq. \textbf  {c}, Per-variant cell depth varies substantially across genes, providing a range of sampling regimes for evaluation (98, 93, 255 and 26 variants; 83.4k, 83.6k, 149.2k and 4.9k cells for TP53, KRAS, GATA1 and JAK1, respectively). \textbf  {d}, Each variant is represented by a 6-dimensional biophysical vector $\theta $ encoding changes in hydrophobicity, side-chain volume and charge, together with fold-core location, functional-switch residue status and hotspot/pathogenic annotation. \textbf  {e}, AllelePerturb-Eval separates prediction quality into direction recovery (Pearson-$\delta $), allele-level discrimination (PDS) and differential-expression fidelity, and defines six split strategies to test random generalization, positional extrapolation, mechanistic extrapolation, cross-gene transfer, low-depth evaluation and compatibility with existing protocols.}}{4}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:1}{{1}{4}{\textbf {AllelePerturb defines perturbation prediction at protein-coding variant resolution.} \textbf {a}, Conceptual distinction between gene-level and protein-coding variant-level perturbation prediction. Gene-level perturbation identity collapses responses from different variants of the same gene into a single averaged effect, whereas AllelePerturb treats a held-out protein-coding variant as the prediction target and asks whether models can distinguish allele-specific responses within the same gene. \textbf {b}, Overview of AllelePerturb-Bench. The benchmark integrates 472 protein-coding variants across four genes and protein architectures (TP53, KRAS, GATA1 and JAK1), spanning three experimental technologies: Perturb-seq, base editing and scSNV-seq. \textbf {c}, Per-variant cell depth varies substantially across genes, providing a range of sampling regimes for evaluation (98, 93, 255 and 26 variants; 83.4k, 83.6k, 149.2k and 4.9k cells for TP53, KRAS, GATA1 and JAK1, respectively). \textbf {d}, Each variant is represented by a 6-dimensional biophysical vector $\theta $ encoding changes in hydrophobicity, side-chain volume and charge, together with fold-core location, functional-switch residue status and hotspot/pathogenic annotation. \textbf {e}, AllelePerturb-Eval separates prediction quality into direction recovery (Pearson-$\delta $), allele-level discrimination (PDS) and differential-expression fidelity, and defines six split strategies to test random generalization, positional extrapolation, mechanistic extrapolation, cross-gene transfer, low-depth evaluation and compatibility with existing protocols}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {All methods recover perturbation direction but cannot rank variant identity.} \textbf  {a}, PDS (cosine) and Pearson-$\delta $ for 20 feature-model combinations with 95\% bootstrap confidence whiskers; no method exceeds the chance PDS of 0.5. \textbf  {b}, Methods cluster in the direction-without-ranking regime. \textbf  {c}, Differential-expression fidelity: direction agreement 78\%, DE-LFC Spearman 0.46, DE overlap 28\%. \textbf  {d}, The dissociation gap follows a per-gene gradient (TP53 0.30, KRAS 0.18, GATA1 0.15, JAK1 0.02). \textbf  {e}, Per-variant scores show stable direction but unstable ranking.}}{6}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:2}{{2}{6}{\textbf {All methods recover perturbation direction but cannot rank variant identity.} \textbf {a}, PDS (cosine) and Pearson-$\delta $ for 20 feature-model combinations with 95\% bootstrap confidence whiskers; no method exceeds the chance PDS of 0.5. \textbf {b}, Methods cluster in the direction-without-ranking regime. \textbf {c}, Differential-expression fidelity: direction agreement 78\%, DE-LFC Spearman 0.46, DE overlap 28\%. \textbf {d}, The dissociation gap follows a per-gene gradient (TP53 0.30, KRAS 0.18, GATA1 0.15, JAK1 0.02). \textbf {e}, Per-variant scores show stable direction but unstable ranking}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Split-half analysis reveals a narrow allele-level measurement window.} \textbf  {a}, Definition of the measurement window from within-variant ($D_\mathrm  {self}$) and variant-to-WT ($D_\mathrm  {null}$) distances. \textbf  {b}, Replicate noise approaches the variant signal: $D_\mathrm  {self}/D_\mathrm  {null}$ = 0.96, 1.01, 0.90 and 0.14 for TP53, KRAS, GATA1 and JAK1. \textbf  {c}, Depth and effect size jointly determine the window. \textbf  {d}, The detection floor extends to gene-level benchmarks (Replogle 55\%, Norman 3\%, Adamson 15\% un-rankable), establishing that the floor is not specific to allele-level tasks (diagnostic; compare Fig.~\ref {fig:5}d). \textbf  {e}, Detection rate rises with depth in a gene-dependent manner.}}{8}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:3}{{3}{8}{\textbf {Split-half analysis reveals a narrow allele-level measurement window.} \textbf {a}, Definition of the measurement window from within-variant ($D_\mathrm {self}$) and variant-to-WT ($D_\mathrm {null}$) distances. \textbf {b}, Replicate noise approaches the variant signal: $D_\mathrm {self}/D_\mathrm {null}$ = 0.96, 1.01, 0.90 and 0.14 for TP53, KRAS, GATA1 and JAK1. \textbf {c}, Depth and effect size jointly determine the window. \textbf {d}, The detection floor extends to gene-level benchmarks (Replogle 55\%, Norman 3\%, Adamson 15\% un-rankable), establishing that the floor is not specific to allele-level tasks (diagnostic; compare Fig.~\ref {fig:5}d). \textbf {e}, Detection rate rises with depth in a gene-dependent manner}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Allele-ranking failure persists across evaluation choices and model interfaces.} \textbf  {a}, PDS across five generalization splits remains at or below chance. \textbf  {b}, Pearson-$\delta $ stays consistently positive across splits. \textbf  {c}, Ranking failure is invariant to the distance function (PDS cosine 0.468, L1 0.454, L2 0.454). \textbf  {d}, The dissociation holds across all genes and splits. \textbf  {e}, Richer features do not close the ranking gap ($\theta $ 0.45, ESM 0.45, ESM+$\theta $ 0.46). \textbf  {f}, Existing methods assume gene-level perturbation identity and fail on allele-resolution input.}}{10}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:4}{{4}{10}{\textbf {Allele-ranking failure persists across evaluation choices and model interfaces.} \textbf {a}, PDS across five generalization splits remains at or below chance. \textbf {b}, Pearson-$\delta $ stays consistently positive across splits. \textbf {c}, Ranking failure is invariant to the distance function (PDS cosine 0.468, L1 0.454, L2 0.454). \textbf {d}, The dissociation holds across all genes and splits. \textbf {e}, Richer features do not close the ranking gap ($\theta $ 0.45, ESM 0.45, ESM+$\theta $ 0.46). \textbf {f}, Existing methods assume gene-level perturbation identity and fail on allele-resolution input}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {A power-aware reporting protocol for allele-resolution perturbation prediction.} \textbf  {a}, Effect size predicts rankability under leave-one-dataset-out cross-validation (mean AUROC 0.974 core, 0.965 all). \textbf  {b}, Discrimination is strong but absolute calibration is weak ($R^2$ = 0.11, mean $|$err$|$ = 26 pp). \textbf  {c}, Rankability is set by the effect-size regime, not by cell count alone. \textbf  {d}, The floor is intrinsic to distributional evaluation at finite depth, shown by native versus depth-matched (n=50) comparison (prescriptive; compare Fig.~\ref {fig:3}d). \textbf  {e}, Recommended power-aware allele-resolution workflow.}}{12}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:5}{{5}{12}{\textbf {A power-aware reporting protocol for allele-resolution perturbation prediction.} \textbf {a}, Effect size predicts rankability under leave-one-dataset-out cross-validation (mean AUROC 0.974 core, 0.965 all). \textbf {b}, Discrimination is strong but absolute calibration is weak ($R^2$ = 0.11, mean $|$err$|$ = 26 pp). \textbf {c}, Rankability is set by the effect-size regime, not by cell count alone. \textbf {d}, The floor is intrinsic to distributional evaluation at finite depth, shown by native versus depth-matched (n=50) comparison (prescriptive; compare Fig.~\ref {fig:3}d). \textbf {e}, Recommended power-aware allele-resolution workflow}{figure.5}{}}
